WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.240 --> 00:00:04.880
Dear Fellow Scholars, this is Two Minute&nbsp;
Papers with Dr. Károly Zsolnai-Fehér.

00:00:04.880 --> 00:00:13.280
Today you will see an absolute banger paper. This&nbsp;
is about how amazingly NVIDIA’s virtual characters&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:13.280 --> 00:00:20.480
can move around after they have trained for 10&nbsp;
years. 10 years? We don’t have 10 years for a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:20.480 --> 00:00:28.480
project! Well, luckily, we don’t have to wait for&nbsp;
10 years. Why is that? I will tell you exactly why&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:28.480 --> 00:00:35.280
in a moment. But, believe me, these folks are&nbsp;
not natural born warriors. They are AI agents&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:35.280 --> 00:00:41.200
that have to train for a long long time&nbsp;
to become so good! So, how does that work?

00:00:41.760 --> 00:00:48.880
Well, first, our candidates are fed a bunch of&nbsp;
basic motions, and then, are dropped into NVIDIA’s&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:48.880 --> 00:00:56.640
Isaac, which is a virtual gym where they can hone&nbsp;
their skills. But, unfortunately, they have none.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:57.280 --> 00:01:03.360
After a week of training, I expected that they&nbsp;
would showcase some amazingly athletic warrior&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:03.360 --> 00:01:12.800
moves, but instead, we got… this. Oh my goodness.&nbsp;
Well, let’s be optimistic and say that they are&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:12.800 --> 00:01:19.040
practicing Judo where the first lesson is&nbsp;
learning how to fall. Yes, let’s say that.

00:01:19.680 --> 00:01:27.360
Then, after two months, we can witness some&nbsp;
improvement. Well, they are not falling,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:27.360 --> 00:01:33.840
and they can do some basic movement.&nbsp;
But, they look like constipated warriors.

00:01:33.840 --> 00:01:41.200
After 2 years, we are starting to see something&nbsp;
that resembles true fight moves. These are not&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:41.200 --> 00:01:49.040
there yet, but they have improved a great deal.&nbsp;
Except this chap. This chap goes like “Sir, I’ve&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:49.040 --> 00:01:56.800
been training for two years, I’ve had enough! And&nbsp;
now, I shall leave… in style.” I wonder what these&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:56.800 --> 00:02:03.600
will look like in 8 more years of training. Well,&nbsp;
hold on to your papers, and let’s see together!

00:02:04.480 --> 00:02:13.360
Oh my! This is absolutely amazing! Now that’s&nbsp;
what I call a bunch of real fighters! See? Time&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:13.360 --> 00:02:20.880
is the answer! It made even our stylish chap take&nbsp;
its training seriously. So, which one is your&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:20.880 --> 00:02:26.160
favorite from here? Did you find some interesting&nbsp;
movements? Let me know in the comments below!

00:02:26.720 --> 00:02:33.520
Now, I promised that we will talk about the ten&nbsp;
year thing. So, did scientist at NVIDIA start this&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:33.520 --> 00:02:42.800
paper in 2012? Well, not quite. This is 10 years&nbsp;
of training, but in a virtual world. However,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:42.800 --> 00:02:49.680
a real world computer simulates this virtual&nbsp;
world and it can do it much quicker than that.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:49.680 --> 00:02:56.880
How much quicker? Well, a powerful machine&nbsp;
can simulate these 10 years not in 10 years,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:56.880 --> 00:03:02.720
but in 10 days. Oh yes! Now&nbsp;
that sounds much better!

00:03:02.720 --> 00:03:09.200
And, we are not done yet. Not even close! When&nbsp;
reading this paper, I was so happy to find out&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:09.200 --> 00:03:13.520
that this new technique also&nbsp;
has four more amazing features.

00:03:14.160 --> 00:03:21.120
One, it works with latent spaces. What is&nbsp;
that? A latent space is a made up place&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:21.120 --> 00:03:27.360
where similar kinds of data are laid out to&nbsp;
be close to each other. In an earlier paper,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:27.360 --> 00:03:34.480
we used such a space to generate beautiful&nbsp;
virtual materials for virtual worlds. NVIDIA&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:35.120 --> 00:03:40.240
uses a latent space to switch between the&nbsp;
motion types that the character now knows,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:40.800 --> 00:03:47.600
and not only that, but their AI also&nbsp;
learned how to weave these motions together,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:47.600 --> 00:03:53.520
even if they were not combined together&nbsp;
in the training data. That is incredible!

00:03:54.240 --> 00:04:01.840
Two, this is my favorite. It has to be. They&nbsp;
not only learned to fall, but in those 10 years,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:01.840 --> 00:04:07.680
they had plenty of opportunity to also learn&nbsp;
to get up. Do you know what this means?&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:08.240 --> 00:04:14.320
Of course, this means the favorite pastime of&nbsp;
the computer graphics researcher. And that is,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:14.320 --> 00:04:20.640
throwing boxes at virtual characters. We like to&nbsp;
say that we are testing whether the character can&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:20.640 --> 00:04:26.320
recover from random perturbations. That&nbsp;
sounds a little more scientific. And,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:26.320 --> 00:04:33.200
these AI agents are passing with flying&nbsp;
colors. Or flying boxes, if you will. Wow.

00:04:34.160 --> 00:04:37.200
Three, the controls are excellent.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:37.760 --> 00:04:43.040
Look. This really has some amazing&nbsp;
potential to be used in virtual worlds,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:43.040 --> 00:04:49.840
because we can even have the character face&nbsp;
one way, and move into a different direction&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:49.840 --> 00:04:56.800
at the same time. More detailed poses can also&nbsp;
be specified. And, what’s more, with this, we&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:56.800 --> 00:05:04.720
can really enter a virtual environment and strike&nbsp;
down these evil pillars with precision. Loving it.

00:05:05.440 --> 00:05:12.480
Four, these motions are synthesized adversarially.&nbsp;
This means that we have a generator neural network&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:12.480 --> 00:05:18.960
creating these new kinds of motions. But, we&nbsp;
connect it to another neural network called the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:18.960 --> 00:05:25.520
discriminator, this watches it and ensures that&nbsp;
the generated motions are similar to the ones in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:25.520 --> 00:05:33.600
the dataset and seem real too. And, as they battle&nbsp;
each other, they also improve together, and in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:33.600 --> 00:05:40.560
the end, we take only the motion types that are&nbsp;
good enough to fool the discriminator. Hopefully,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:40.560 --> 00:05:47.840
these are good enough to fool the human eye too.&nbsp;
And, as you see, the results speak for themselves.

00:05:47.840 --> 00:05:49.840
If we wouldn’t be doing it this way,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:49.840 --> 00:05:53.120
here is what we would get if we&nbsp;
trained these agents from scratch.

00:05:53.680 --> 00:06:00.400
And, yes, while we are talking about training.&nbsp;
This did not start out well at all. Imagine&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:00.400 --> 00:06:08.000
if scientists at NVIDIA quit after just 1 week of&nbsp;
training, which is about 30 minutes in real time.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:08.000 --> 00:06:14.720
These results are not too promising, are they?&nbsp;
But, they still kept going. And the result was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:14.720 --> 00:06:22.240
this! That is excellent life advice right there,&nbsp;
and, also, this is an excellent opportunity for us&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:22.240 --> 00:06:28.320
to invoke the The Third Law Of Papers.&nbsp;
Not the first, the third one! This says&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:28.320 --> 00:06:36.720
that a bad researcher fails 100% of the time,&nbsp;
while a good one only fails 99% of the time.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:37.280 --> 00:06:42.480
Hence, what you see here is always&nbsp;
just 1% of the work that was done.

00:06:43.200 --> 00:06:50.480
And, this is done by NVIDIA, so I am sure that&nbsp;
we will see this deployed in real world projects,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:50.480 --> 00:06:55.920
where these amazing agents will get democratized&nbsp;
by putting it into the hands of all of us.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:56.480 --> 00:07:00.960
What a time to be alive! So,&nbsp;
does this get your mind going?&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:00.960 --> 00:07:04.400
What would you use this for? Let&nbsp;
me know in the comments below!

00:08:01.760 --> 00:08:05.920
Thanks for watching and for your generous&nbsp;
support, and I'll see you next time!

